────────────────────────── THE RESULT ──────────────────────────────────

P5 COLD START — PASS. The prediction the whole fix stands on.
  first correct ls-remote  13 s after the pod was deleted
  first COMPLETE clone     29 s after it (16.0 s for the clone itself)
  refused attempts         0
  runcd, same leg:         REFUSED TO SERVE — exit 78, crash-loop, 10 restarts

  NOT VACUOUS. The repository had folded hard before the delete: the
  batch log carries 13 fold commits, each superseding real inputs —
    seq  12: −9 packs     seq  49: −32     seq  70: −64    seq  72: −206
    seq  78: −69          seq  83: −72     seq  88: −63    seq  93: −75
    seq  99: −68          seq 106: −64     seq 112: −80    seq 118: −72
    seq 124: −75
  and it carried P9's 48 pushes and P2's 931 before the pod went away.
  A P5 against a repository that never folded would prove nothing; that
  is the trap this rig has fallen into before.

P11 UNDO — PASS. Undo point seq 1 holds the pre-force tip a7034cc3 and
  every pack it names is still in the bucket (1 of them).

P10 BUCKET CUT OFF — PASS, and this is Inv_AckedIsDurable on the wire:
  a read at +5 s               rc=0 (still serving from local packs)
  readiness                    not ready after 79 s
  a push during the outage     TIMED OUT — not acknowledged
  recovery                     push told ok, pod ready, restarts 0 → 0
  No push was acked that was not durable.

────────────────────── THE NUMBERS, AND ONE COST ───────────────────────

                          runce (aaffe335)        runcd (d54b29cf)
  P9  48×8 MiB            35 s, median 0.56 s     34 s
  P9  bytes uploaded      705.5 MB = 1.75x        378.5 MB = 0.94x   ← WORSE
  P2  rate                931 acked = 15.5/s      752 acked = 12.5/s
  P2  bytes               1.136 GB = 1.22 MB/push 1.44 GB = 1.92 MB/push
  P7  1 GiB clone solo    17.0 s                  20.2 s
  P7  8 concurrent        82 s, 100 MiB/s agg     71 s
  P12 ref ladder (s)      0.20 / 0.29 / 0.40      0.28 / 0.43 / 0.74
  P12 decay across it     2.0x                    2.6x
  resident at the end     2.22 GB / 3221 objects  (not comparable: fewer legs)

PREDICTION 3 WAS WRONG ABOUT WHERE THE COST LANDED. I pre-registered
that the strict rule would cost BYTES, since keeping more input packs
named means less is collected, and I predicted it would show in P2.
P2 got BETTER (1.92 → 1.22 MB per push). P9's amplification is what
regressed, 0.94x → 1.75x — for the same 402.7 MB of payload, forge
uploaded 705.5 MB instead of 378.5 MB. The mechanism is the one
predicted, in the leg not predicted: an input the roll-up does not
fully hold stays named, so a later fold takes it as an input again and
re-uploads its contents.
  CONFOUND, STATED: in runce P9 ran FIRST, against a fresh repository;
  in runcd it ran after P1 and P4. Different repository state drives
  different fold planning. This is cross-run and cross-order, so it
  bounds the cost rather than measuring it. A same-cluster A/B of the
  two fold rules is the experiment that would settle it, and it has
  not been run.

P12 is cross-run too — there is no in-run control arm, so the ladder's
improvement (2.6x → 2.0x decay) is suggestive, not established. The
controlled version of that question is the local rig, which has a plain
bare-repository control; it is set up (prereg-packrefs.md, two binaries
differing ONLY in the two pack-refs call sites, verified by content) and
was not run because the cluster took priority.

foldsRefused: 0 on every syncer, and no "the fold keeps N input pack(s)
named" line in any log.
  LIMITATION: that counter is per PROCESS and P5 deletes the pod. The
  13 folds happened before the restart; their process's counter and
  logs are gone. So this is evidence about the successor's life only,
  not about the folds that matter. READ THE COUNTER BEFORE THE
  DESTRUCTIVE LEG — a rig fix for next time.

──────────────────────── TWO RIG DEFECTS FOUND ─────────────────────────

1. P5 SILENTLY DEPENDS ON P7. P5 clones `agent/p7-$RUN`; run without
   P7 in the same invocation it reports INCONCLUSIVE ("not present").
   The first pass of this drill dropped P7 to save time and lost the
   one leg it existed to run. The dependency is not documented in the
   leg list.
2. P11 CANNOT RUN THROUGH THE DOOR ON A DEFAULT REPOSITORY. The door's
   branch policy refuses the non-fast-forward update ("remote rejected
   … non-fast-forward update to refs/heads/agent/…") — correct
   behaviour, but it makes the undo leg INCONCLUSIVE by construction.
   It needs a repository with `allowNonFastForward: ["agent/*"]`; this
   run created a third FlintRepo `undo` for it.

